window.PLD=window.PLD||{};PLD["R-tx-2025-01-21"]={"AD-001-25":"Resolution No. AD-001-25\nFrom: Administration Committee.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nRENEWAL OF CONTRACT WITH ENTERPRISE TRAINING SOLUTIONS, INC. FOR\ne-LEARNING/DISTANCE LEARNING TRAINING SERVICES\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara is required to comply with new NYS requirements to provide annual training to all employees and separate higher level training for supervisors/managers on the Niagara County Sexual Harassment Prevention Policy, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara has determined that the most efficient means to comply with this new requirement is to provide the majority of the training with the use of technology whereby employees conduct interactive online training in a self-paced approach, on an annualized basis as required by regulation, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara has selected to engage in a professional services contract with Enterprise Training Solutions, Inc. which is on State contract through the Office of General Services (OGS), award # 22883 for Group 73016 - e-Learning/Distance Leaming Training Services (Statewide) for the time period of January 15, 2025 through January 14, 2028, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara wishes to renew the contract and continue to partner with Enterprise Training solutions, Inc. for an additional 3-year contract, and\nWHEREAS, the annual fee for the aforementioned online trainings, and a third training topic (to be determined) of the new contract term shall continue to be $16,000 each year and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the contract, the County Attorney will review the contract for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the contract may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","AD-002-25":"Resolution No. AD-002-25\nFrom: Administration Committee.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nNIAGARA COUNTY REAL PROPERTY TAX SERVICES\nEXTENSION OF REAL PROPERTY TAX SERVICES CONSULTANT AGREEMENT\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara has entered into a Consulting Agreement with a consultant for services of real property appraisal, equalization and assessment, and tax rate apportionment for the Niagara County Real Property Tax Services Department, and\nWHEREAS, the current agreement was entered into with John Shoemaker, and\nWHEREAS, the current agreement is dated April 1, 2024 and has a term period until December 31, 2024, and\nWHEREAS, the County wishes to extend the agreement with John Shoemaker for a one-year extension period,\nnow, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature has determined that the services to be provided by the consultant requires a high degree of specialized skill constituting \"professional services\" and hereby waives any requirement that additional proposals must be obtained or solicited, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that County Attorney will draft a contract for a one-year period, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the contract shall be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Purchasing Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","AD-003-25":"Resolution No. AD-003-25\nFrom: Administration Committee.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nAUTHORIZATION FOR REAL PROPERTY TAX\nREFUNDS/CORRECTIONS - 2025\nWHEREAS, New York State Real Property Tax Law, sections 554 and section 556, provides for an administrative correction of error process that assists property owners in obtaining refunds and/or corrections for real property tax payments or billings, and\nWHEREAS, certain clerical errors, unlawful entries and errors in essential fact, as defined in the New York State Real Property Tax Law, are permitted to be corrected by a taxing jurisdiction based upon the recommendation of the Director of Real Property Tax Services, and\nWHEREAS, the taxing jurisdiction can delegate, by resolution, on an annual basis, to an official who is empowered to authorize payment of bills without prior audit, the authority to perform the duties of the tax levying body in regard to Real Property Tax Law, Sections 554 and 556, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Treasurer is hereby, delegated the authority to perform the duties of the tax levying body during 2025 when the Real Property Tax Director's recommended refund and/or correction is twenty five hundred dollars or less, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that on or before the fifteenth day of each month, the Niagara County Treasurer shall submit a report to the Niagara County Legislature of the refunds and or corrections processed during the preceding month, containing the name of each recipient, the location of the property, and the amount of the refund.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","AD-004-25":"Resolution No. AD-004-25\nFrom: Administration Committee.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nAGREEMENT BETWEEN NIAGARA COUNTY AND BRIAN PARISI COPIER SYSTEMS INC.\nTO PROVIDE MULTIFUNCTION COPIER SERVICES\nWHEREAS, an invitation for bid (IFB# 2019-02) for Lease of Multifunction Copying, Faxing and Scanning Devices was issued and publicly opened by the Purchasing Department on July 9, 2019, and\nWHEREAS, after conducting an evaluation of all proposals, the County awarded Brian Parisi Copiers Systems Inc. as suppliers of the multifunction copier services, and\nWHEREAS, the County entered into an agreement with Brian Parisi Copier Systems Inc., for five years, and\nWHEREAS, the current agreement will expire February 24, 2025, and\nWHEREAS, the IFB# 2019-02 allotted for two one-year extensions, and\nWHEREAS, the County desires to extend the contract for the first one-year extension allotted, now, therefore,\nbe it\nRESOLVED that the County enter into a one-year extension agreement with Brian Parisi Copier Systems Inc. to provide multifunction copier services, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Agreement may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CS-001-25":"Resolution No. CS-001-25\nFrom: Community Services and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nBUDGET MODIFICATION -ACCEPT MEDICAL RESERVE CORPS FUNDS\nEMERGENCY PLANNING-DEPARTMENT OF HEAL TH\nWHEREAS, the Emergency Planning Program of the Niagara County Department of Health provides services, information and training to protect the health and safety of the citizens of Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, the National Association of County and City Health Officials has awarded our Emergency Planning Program Medical Reserve Corps Operational Readiness Award funds to assist in preparedness of local Medical Reserve Corps (MRC) capabilities from 12/1/24 through 9/29/2025, and\nWHEREAS the Department of Health requests authorization to accept funds, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, the budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE REVENUE:\nCM.20.4189.406 44489.24  Other Health MRC  $ 5,000.00\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nCM.20.4189.406 74375.01  Advertising & Promotion  $  500.00\nCM.20.4189.406 74250.03  Printing/duplicating  475.00\nCM.20.4189.406 74300.03  Mileage  300.00\nCM.20.4189.406 74750.02  Supplies/Materials  3,725.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CS-002-25":"Resolution No. CS-002-25\nFrom: Community Services and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nBID AW ARD FOR PROFESSIONAL ENGINEER\nENVIRONMENTAL DIVISION-DEPARTMENT OF HEALTH\nWHEREAS, the Environmental Division of the Niagara County Department of Health provides essential services, information and training to protect the health and safety of citizens of Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, a Professional Engineer is required to approve sewer, septic, water, and subdivision construction, and\nWHEREAS, the Environmental Division of the Department of Health is experiencing the retirement of our last Professional Engineer on staff this month, and\nWHEREAS, the position is being held open with no available applicants, and\nWHEREAS, the Purchasing Department advertised for bids and publicly opened and read bids on November 21,2024,and\nWHEREAS, the New York State Department of Audit and Control as, in the past, requested the Purchasing Agent to refer the results of the bid to the Niagara County Legislature for its approval, and\nWHEREAS, Public Health management has interviewed and considered both bids, and\nWHEREAS, funds are available in the 2025 Public Health budget, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the low bid, as outlined on the attached result sheet, be awarded to LaBella Associates, DPC.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CS-003-25":"Resolution No. CS-003-25\nFrom: Community Services and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nABOLISH ACCOUNT CLERICAL III/CREATE & FILL ACCOUNT CLERICAL IV POSITION-\nFISCAL DIVISION- DEPARTMENT OF HEALTH\nWHEREAS, the Fiscal Division of Niagara County Department of Health provides the Public Health Department with essential support services, and\nWHEREAS, the duties of Account Clerical III have been reviewed finding Account Clerical IV duties better represents increased required scope of support duties of this position, and\nWHEREAS, the minimal increase cost will be offset with a decrease in travel, conference, and\nWHEREAS, availability of fiscal support is a priority for division leadership and growing number of complex grants, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that effective February 2, 2025 Account Clerical III (position# 601), CSEA Job Group 6 at $44,835 per year, be abolished, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that effective February 3, 2025 the position of Account Clerical IV, CSEA Job Group 8 at $48,196 per year, be created and filled, and be it further\nRESOLVED, the following budget modification be made:\nINCREASE REVENUE:\nA.20.4010.000 43401.00  State Aid  $  864\nDECREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.20.4010.000 74300.01  Travel Conference  $ 1,536\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.20.4010.000 71010.00  position TBD  $ 1,966\nA.20.4010.000 78200.00  FICA  150\nA.20.4010.000 78100.00  Retirement  243\nA.20.4010.000 78300.00  Worker's Comp  41\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CS-004-25":"Resolution No. CS-004-25\nFrom: Community Services and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nNIAGARA COUNTY EMPLOYMENT & TRAINING DEPARTMENT BUDGET MODIFICATION\nACCEPT $250,000 RENEWABLE ENERGY TRAINING INITIATIVE (RETI) FUNDING 2025\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Local Workforce Development Board (NCWDB) has been awarded $250,000 under the Renewable Energy Training Initiative (RETI), and\nWHEREAS, this state funded program is allocated by the New York Power Authority (NYPA) working in cooperation with the New York State Department of Labor's (NYDOL), Office of Just Energy Transition (OJET) to support clean energy and workforce development training, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Employment & Training (NCET) Department will manage and administer these training and supportive funds as outlined in the Subrecipient Agreement, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the NCWDB and NCET are authorized to accept this award, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the 2025 NCET Department Budget be modified as follows:\nINCREASE REVENUE:\nCD.29.6291.000 43089.xx  State Aid Other, RETI  $250,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nCD.29.6291.000 74500.01  Contractual Expenses  $250,000\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-002-25":"Resolution No. CSS-002-25\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nBUDGET MODIFICATION - ACCEPT FY24 STONEGARDEN GRANT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has been notified by the New York State Department of Homeland Security and Emergency Services that it has been awarded $107,650 under the FY2024 Operation Stonegarden Program, whose funding is provided by the U.S. Department of Homeland Security and Emergency Services, and\nWHEREAS, Operation Stonegarden has been awarded to the Niagara County Sheriffs Office for many years for expenses to assist the County in conducting border centric, intelligence driven operations with the goal of reduction or elimination of threat, risk and vulnerability along our nation's borders, and\nWHEREAS, the performance period for the grant is September I, 2024 through August 31, 2027, and\nWHEREAS, the 2025 budget needs to be amended to accept the revenue, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the FY24 Operation Stonegarden Grant may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE ESTIMATED REVENUE:\nA.17.3645.000 44305.02  Civil Defense Homeland Security  $107,650\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3645.000 71050.00  Overtime Expense  $18,166\nA.17.3645.000 72100.21  Law Enforcement Equipment  43,287\nA.l 7.3645.000 74400.09  Payments Other Agencies  42,197\nA.17.3645.000 78100.00  Retirement Expense  2,442\nA.17.3645.000 78200.00  FICA Expense  1,224\nA.17.3645.000 78300.00  Worker's Compensation Expense  334\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-003-25":"Resolution No. CSS-003-25\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nBUDGET MODIFICATION - FY2020 EDWARD BYRNE MEMORIAL JUSTICE ASSISTANCE LAW\nENFORCEMENT EQUIPMENT GRANT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature passed Resolution #CSS-068-24 on 8/6/24 to accept a FY2020 Edward Byrne Memorial Justice Assistance Law Enforcement Equipment Grant through New York State Division of Criminal Justice Services in the amount of $602,000 for the performance period 7 /1/24 - 6/30/25, and\nWHEREAS, the grant has a budget modification to add the purchase and upfitting of a patrol vehicle due to actual costs being lower than the original budgeted amounts, and\nWHEREAS, the budget modification must be accepted by the County and changes made to enable purchase of the vehicle, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the FY2020 Edward Byrne Memorial Justice Assistance Law Enforcement Equipment Grant budget modification may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nDECREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3645.000 72100.05 17GRTJAGLEE20  Computer Equipment  $9,757\nA.17.3645.000 72100.15 17GRTJAGLEE20  Communications Equipment  43,233\nA.17.3645.000 72100.21 17GRTJAGLEE20  Law Enforcement Equipment  710\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3645.000 72100.12 17GRTJAGLEE20  Mach & Equip, Cars  $45,000\nA.17.3645.000 74800.11 17GRTJAGLEE20  Vehicle Maintenance  8,700\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-004-25":"Resolution No. CSS-004-25\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nBUDGET MODIFICATION - POLICE TRAFFIC SERVICES GRANT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature passed Resolution #CSS-115-24 to accept the Police Traffic Services Grant from the Governor's Traffic Safety Committee for the period October 1, 2024 through September 30, 2025 in the amount of $25,065, and\nWHEREAS, this grant is a continuation of an existing program and is fully funded by the New York State Governor's Traffic Safety Committee, and\nWHEREAS, the funds are used for traffic enforcement overtime with the goal of increasing seat belt usage and reducing dangerous driving behaviors in an effort to reduce serious injuries and deaths from traffic crashes in Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, the 2025 Budget needs to be modified in order to utilize these funds, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE ESTIMATED REVENUE:\nA.17.3110.000 44389.09  Other Public Safety Traffic  $25,065\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3110.000 71050.00  Overtime Expense  $25,065\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-005-25":"Resolution No. CSS-005-25\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nBUDGET MODIFICATION - SHSP 2024 HOMELAND SECURITY GRANT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Emergency Management Office entered into FY24 State Homeland Security Grant Program I SLETPP grant through New York State Department of Homeland Security and Emergency Services which was accepted by the County in November of 2024, and\nWHEREAS, the SHSP 2024 grant includes funds for Operational Overtime for counter terrorism patrol of potential targets, and\nWHEREAS, the SHSP 2024 operational overtime and fringe funds need to be added to the Sheriffs Office 2025 budget in order to be utilized, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE ESTIMATED REVENUE:\nA.l 7.3645.000 44305.02  Civil Defense Homeland Security  $13,551\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3645.000 71050.00  Overtime Expense  $ 10,658\nA.17.3645.000 78100.00  Retirement Expense  1,848\nA.17.3645.000 78200.00  FICA Expense  815\nA.17.3645.000 78300.00  Workers Comp Expense  230\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-006-25":"Resolution No. CSS-006-25\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nBUDGET MODIFICATION- STOP DWI FOUNDATION GRANT\nWHEREAS, the New York State STOP-DWI Foundation, Inc. has notified the Niagara County Sheriffs Office ofan award of$35,000 in grant funds available to use for High Visibility Engagement Campaigns this year, and\nWHEREAS, the grant funds are utilized for enforcement efforts by multiple law enforcement agencies during peak holidays with the goal of reducing the number of crashes, injuries and deaths on Niagara County roads, and\nWHEREAS, the 2025 budget was created with $28,000 in STOP DWI Foundation funds as was originally communicated, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to modify the 2025 budget to utilize the additional $7,000, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE ESTIMATED REVENUE:\nA.17.3315.000 44389.09  Other Public Safety Traffic  $7,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3315.000 74400.09  Miscellaneous Expenses Payments Other Agencies  $7,000\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-007-25":"Resolution No. CSS-007-25\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nBUDGET MODIFICATION - DCJS LAW ENFORCEMENT EQUIPMENT GRANT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature passed Resolution CSS-096-24 on October 15, 2024 to accept the New York State FY202 l Edward Byrne Memorial Justice Assistance Grant in the amount of $84,800 through the New York State Division of Criminal Justice Services for the period for 10/1/24 to 9/30/25, and\nWHEREAS, the grant was accepted after the 2025 budget was created, and\nWHEREAS, the New York State FY2021 Edward Byrne Memorial Justice Assistance Grant in the amount of $84,800 has to be added to the Sheriff's Office 2025 budget in order to utilize the funds, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE ESTIMATED REVENUE:\nA.17.3645.000 44305.02  Civil Defense Homeland Security  $84,800\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3645.000 72100.21  Law Enforcement Equipment  $77,300\nA.17.3645.000 74750.05  Law Enforcement Supplies  7,500\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-008-25":"Resolution No. CSS-008-25\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nCREATE AND FILL BUILDING MAINTENANCE MECHANIC POSITION\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has been notified that a Building Maintenance Mechanic who has been on a medical leave since August 2024 does not anticipate being able to return to work, and\nWHEREAS, the extended leave put a large stress on the Niagara County Jail operations due to the high volume of maintenance needs for the aging facility, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that a temporary Building Maintenance Mechanic position be created and filled effective 2/2/2025 at AFSCME Building Maintenance Mechanic Step 1 at an hourly rate of $22.67 /hour, and, be it further\nRESOLVED, that the temporary position will be abolished upon the current Building Maintenance Mechanic position vacancy, and, be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nDECREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3150.000 71010.00  Position Expense  43,164\nPosn # 4492\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3150.000 71010.00  Position Expense  43,164\nPosn#NEW\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-009-25":"Resolution No. CSS-009-25\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nCREATE AND FILL TEMPORARY SHERIFF DISPATCHER POSITION\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has been notified of the extended military leave of a Sheriff Dispatcher who has been on military leave since May 2023 and has orders through September 2025, and\nWHEREAS, the extended leave puts a large stress on the Emergency Dispatch operations causing excessive overtime, and\nWHEREAS, due to the lengthy military leave that is expected to continue, it is necessary to create a temporary Sheriff Dispatcher position to be funded with savings from the position on military leave, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that a temporary Sheriff Dispatcher position be created and filled effective 2/2/2025 at DSA Dispatcher Step 1 at an hourly rate of $20.37 /hour, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the temporary position will be abolished upon any Sheriff Dispatcher opening or the return of the employee from military leave, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nDECREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3020.000 71010.00  Position Expense  $38,785\nPosn # 4406\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3020.000 71010.00  Position Expense  $38,785\nPosn#NEW\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-010-25":"Resolution No. CSS-010-25\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nCREATE AND FILL TWO TEMPORARY CORRECTION OFFICER POSITIONS\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has been notified of the extended military leave of a Correction Officer who has been on military leave since April 2022 and has orders through September 2025, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has been notified of an employee currently on extended 207C leave who will be retiring and not returning to work, and\nWHEREAS, the extended leaves put a large stress on the Niagara County Jail operations causing excessive overtime, and\nWHEREAS, due to the lengthy military leave that is expected to continue and the anticipated retirement, it is necessary to create two temporary Correction Officer positions to be funded with savings from the position on military leave, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that two temporary Correction Officer positions be created and filled effective 2/2/2025 at DSA Correction Officer Step 2 at an hourly rate of $26.02/hour, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the temporary positions will be abolished upon the Correction Officer's retirement or the return of the employee from military leave, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nDECREASE APPROPRlATIONS:\nA.17.3150.000 71010.00  Position Expense  $57,870\nPosn# 3426\nINCREASE APPROPRJATIONS:\nA.17.3150.000 71010.00  Position Expense  $49,543\nPosn#NEW\nAl 7.3150.000 71010.00  Position Expense  8,327\nPosn#NEW\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-011-25":"Resolution No. CSS-011-25\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nCREATE AND FILL TWO TEMPORARY DEPUTY SHERIFF POSITIONS\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has been notified of the retirement of a Criminal Investigator and a Deputy Sheriff in March and April of 2025, and\nWHEREAS, the next Niagara County Law Enforcement Academy will start in March of 2025 and the following Academy will not start until September of 2025, and\nWHEREAS, new Deputy Sheriffs go through a six month Law Enforcement Academy, and then two months of Field Training before they are able to operate on their own, and\nWHEREAS, due to the lengthy training needed for a new Deputy Sherriff it is important to start new Deputies in the March Academy in anticipation of these retirements, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that two temporary Deputy Sheriff positions be created and filled effective 3/2/2025 at PBA Deputy Sheriff Step 2 at an hourly rate of $31.26/hour, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the temporary positions will be abolished upon the Deputy Sheriff vacancies from the retirements, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nDECREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3110.000 71010.00  Position Expense  $54,518\nPosn # 10034\nA.17.3110.000 71010.00  Position Expense  54,518\nPosn# 362\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3110.000 71010.00  Position Expense  $54,518\nPosn#NEW\nA.17.3110.000 71010.00  Position Expense  54,518\nPosn#NEW\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-012-25":"Resolution No. CSS-012-25\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nEXPENDITURE OF CELLULAR E911 FUNDS FOR OLCOTT COMMUNICATIONS TOWER\nPOWER PROJECT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature passed a Local Law on 11/15/17, which established an E911 surcharge for cellular phones to establish a dedicated revenue to be used for future payments for system costs, eligible wireless 911 service costs, or other costs associated with the administration, design, and enhanced wireless service costs serving Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, such expenditures require the authorization of the Niagara County Legislature, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office and the Niagara County Office of Emergency Services have identified an issue with the current power source for the Olcott Communications Tower, and\nWHEREAS, the power to the Olcott Communications Tower is currently on a shared power line from a building that Niagara County currently leases, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office will be pursuing a solution to provide independent power to the Olcott Communications Tower, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Sheriffs Office and Niagara County Office of Emergency Services be authorized to spend up to $150,000 in cellular E911 funds for the necessary project to provide power directly to the Olcott Communications Tower, following Purchasing Guidelines\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-013-25":"Resolution No. CSS-013-25\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nFEDERAL EQUITABLE SHARING ANNUAL RECERTIFICATION\nWHEREAS, the United States Department of Justice requires that the Niagara County Sheriffs Office file an annual Federal Equitable Sharing Program Agreement and Certification Report in order to remain eligible to submit requests for federal forfeited property or proceeds, and\nWHEREAS, the Agreement is entered into by the United States Department of Justice, the County of Niagara and the Niagara County Sheriffs Office, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Sheriffs Office be authorized to continue to participate in the Federal Equitable Sharing Program, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the United States Department of Justice Federal Equitable Sharing Agreement may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-014-25":"Resolution No. CSS-014-25\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nJAIL HEALTHCARE BUSINESS ASSOCIATE\nAND QUALIFIED SERVICE ORGANIZATION AGREEMENT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office utilizes PrimeCare Medical as a third-party contractor to provide medical care for incarcerated individuals, and\nWHEREAS, utilizing a third-party provider requires an updated Business Associate Agreement and Qualified Service Organization Agreement (\"BAA/QSOA\"), and\nWHEREAS, the BAA/QSOA specifies the manner in which incarcerated individuals' medical information will be protected and the rights of the County to access and disclose medical information in order to abide by all applicable federal and state laws and regulations that govern the privacy and confidentiality of secure patient health information, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review and approval, the Business Associate Agreement and Qualified Service Organization Agreement may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-015-25":"Resolution No. CSS-015-25\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nSOLE SOURCE STANDARDIZATION NATIONAL GRID\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office is starting a project to provide dedicated power to the Olcott Communications Tower, and\nWHEREAS, National Grid is the only utility company which can provide electrical service in the area, and\nWHEREAS, the Sheriffs Office engages in other projects with the Communications Towers which require service from the electricity utility company designated for the area in which the tower is located, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has received an estimate from National Grid for the Olcott Communications Tower to replace an electrical pole, install a riser, overhead ratio transformer, cutout, cutout blade and single-phase underground primary in an amount less than $20,000, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that for reasons of efficiency and economy, pursuant to General Municipal Law 103(5), there is a need for sole source purchase and standardization for the Niagara County Sheriffs Office in the provision of electrical service to the Olcott Communications Tower and future projects in which National Grid is the power utility company for the area, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that it is hereby, determined that National Grid is the sole provider of electrical utility service for the Olcott area and other areas in Niagara County and that the Sheriffs Office is hereby authorized to purchase the electrical service work from National Grid in all areas where National Grid is the sole electrical utility company without the need to solicit for alternate proposals, such purchase to be made in consultation with the Purchasing Department and in accordance with budgetary procedures, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, all orders with National Grid may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-016-25":"Resolution No. CSS-016-25\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nWAIVER OF RESIDENCY REQUIREMENT\nCONFLICT ATTORNEY\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Conflicts Office & Assigned Counsel has permission to fill presently vacant Conflict Attorney positions under position numbers: 13681; 13814; 13960; 13961; 19364; 13969 and 13970; these are important positions that are required to provide legal representation of indigent persons entitled to public defense who cannot be represented by the Public Defender's Office due to a conflict of interest, and\nWHEREAS, due to recent challenges with recruitment efforts of Niagara County residents that are experienced criminal and family Court trial attorneys for these vacant positions of Conflict Attorney, and\nWHEREAS, the Conflicts Office & Assigned Counsel has made diligent and sincere efforts to recruit highly qualified candidates for these vacant positions from within Niagara County, including multiple advertisements and postings for an extended period of time, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that a Waiver of Residency from the policy to hire only Niagara County residents be approved to fill these vacant position numbers: 13681; 13814; 13960; 13961; 19364; 13969 and 13970 of Conflict Attorney when the most qualified applicant or only qualified candidates resides outside Niagara County.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-017-25":"Resolution No. CSS-017-25\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nPROBATION 2024-2025 ACCEPT IGNITION INTERLOCK DEVICE\nMONITORING PROGRAM A WARD\nWHEREAS, the New York State Division of Criminal Justice Services has made an award to Niagara County for the Ignition Interlock Device Monitoring Program for term dates October 1, 2024 through September 30, 2025 and,\nWHEREAS, the Grant is being renewed in the amount of $21,220, and said funds are used to offset expenses for monitoring Ignition Interlock Device installation by offenders, and the revenue has been budgeted for in the 2024 and 2025 budgets, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the grant agreement, the County Attorney will review the agreement for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the grant agreement may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-018-25":"Resolution No. CSS-018-25\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nEMERGENCY SERVICES\nINTER-MUNICIPAL TRAILER TRANSFER AGREEMENT\nWHEREAS, Niagara County Department of Emergency Services currently possesses a 2003 Haulmark trailer (Fleet # 50248-03), previously used for the HazMat team which the county wishes to declare as surplus property, and\nWHEREAS, the City of Lockport Fire Department has a need for a trailer matching the above description, and\nWHEREAS, an Inter-Municipal Agreement between the County of Niagara and the City of Lockport Fire Department has been drafted by the County Attorney's Office for a transfer of aforementioned 2003 Haulmark trailer, new therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the County of Niagara declares the 2003 Haulmark trailer to be surplus property and authorizes an agreement with the City of Lockport Fire Department to transfer ownership of the 2003 Haulmark trailer, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the agreement may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-019-25":"Resolution No. CSS-019-25\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\n2025 BUDGET MODIFICATION - FIRE COORDINATOR'S OFFICE\nACCEPT PAYMENT FOR A HAZ-MAT INCIDENT\nWHEREAS, the County Haz-Mat team responded an incident which resulted in depleted supplies that cannot be reused, and\nWHEREAS, under New York State's Navigation and Environmental Conservation Laws, the responsible party is liable for all costs associated with containment, cleanup and removal of spilled and contaminated materials, and\nWHEREAS, the Fire Coordinator's Office invoiced and received payment in December 2024 in the amount of $10,747.76 for the Industrial Methylene spill that HazMat responded to on 9/30/24, and\nWHEREAS, the funds were collected and recorded in 2024 and the Fire Coordinator's Office will need to replenish the supplies used by the Haz-Mat team in 2025, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following 2025 budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE APPROPRIATED FUND BALANCE\nA 40599.00  Appropriated Fund Balance  $ 10,747.76\nINCREASE APPROPRIATION:\nA.19.3410.000 72100.33  M&E, Hazmat Equipment  $ 8,800.00\nA.19.3410.000 74750.10  Supplies, Gen Hazmat Inventory  1,947.76\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","ED-002-25":"Resolution No. ED-002-25\nFrom: Economic Development Committee.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nFIXING DATE AND NOTICE FOR THE SECOND PUBLIC HEARING\nFOR BRENT INDUSTRIES, LLC COMMUNITY DEVELOPMENT BLOCK GRANT\nWHEREAS, the County Legislature wishes to hear public comments relating to the recently completed Community Development Block Grant (\"CDBG\") project supporting the grant funding provided to Brent Industries, LLC at 9745 Rochester Road, within the Town of Royalton, and\nWHEREAS, the CDBG program is administered by the New York State Office of Community\nRenewal(\"OCR\"), and provides resources to eligible local govermnents for housing, economic development, public facilities, public infrastructure, and planning activities, with the principal purpose of benefitting low/moderate income persons, and\nWHEREAS, OCR requires Niagara County to hold a public hearing to provide information to the public and to consider citizens' comments regarding administration of CDBG funded project to help make determinations for the future use of CDBG funds, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature shall conduct a public hearing whereat all interested parties shall be heard, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Public Hearing shall be held at the Legislative Chambers, Courthouse, Lockport, New York on the 18th day of February, 2025 at 5:50 p.m., and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Clerk of the Legislature, at least eight days in advance of such hearing, shall post a notice on the Niagara County Legislature website, and shall publish such notice once in the Lockport Union Sun & Journal, and the Niagara Gazette.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","ED-003-25":"Resolution No. ED-003-25\nFrom: Economic Development Committee.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nAPPROVAL OF LOW COST POWER BENEFIT TO VIRTUAL POLYMER\nCOMPOUNDS, LLC UNDER THE AGREEMENT FOR THE SALE & PURCHASE OF\nNIAGARA PROJECT POWER & ENERGY (ASPNPPE)\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara entered into the Host Community Relicensing and Settlement Agreement (\"HCRSA\") on June 25, 2005, and\nWHEREAS, the HCRSA entitles the County of Niagara to 9 megawatts of low cost power allocation (at 70% load) from the New York Power Authority, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara has made the use of this low cost power allocation for economic development purposes as the top priority for that allocation, and\nWHEREAS, there is presently an Agreement for the Sale and Purchase of Niagara Project Power and Energy (\"ASPNPPE\") that has been approved by the County of Niagara and NYP A and the Governor of the State of New York, and\nWHEREAS, the ASPNPPE specifically provides that the County of Niagara could use its low cost power allocation for economic development purposes, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara has taken the necessary actions to fully implement the benefits contained in the HCRSA and the ASPNPPE, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara established the Empower Niagara Board by Resolution ED-021-07 to review applications for the use of low cost power for economic development by reviewing applications and to make recommendations to the Legislature of Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara has previously awarded to Virtual Polymer Compounds, LLC for this same project under resolution ED-002-22, 200 kw (at 70% load factor), which agreement will expire on January 31, 2025 and for which Virtual Polymer Compounds, LLC has met each part of the previous application, and\nWHEREAS, the Empower Niagara Board has reviewed the extension request of Virtual Polymer Compounds, LLC for the use of low cost power to allow it to remain competitive in Niagara County and Virtual Polymer Compounds, LLC has met or exceeded each part of the previous application, including an increase in its workforce from 38 to 53 employees, an expansion of production capacity through new equipment and processes that lower costs and improve efficiency, and a significant capital investment which has led to an increase in sales from $4.8 million in 2021 to $8 million in 2023, and\nWHEREAS, Virtual Polymer Compounds, LLC has implemented several energy efficiency projects, including elimination of two large inefficient air handling/heating units, installation of an energy management system and the conversion to LED lighting throughout the entire complex, and\nWHEREAS, Virtual Polymer Compounds, LLC has purchased nearly $250,000 in services from fifteen Niagara County companies, which has expanded its economic impact to Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, Virtual Polymer Compounds, LLC has met or exceeded all the criteria qualifying its original project for consideration and the Empower Niagara Board is recommending approval of a three-year extension of the agreement, and\nWHEREAS, the demand usage of Virtual Polymer Compounds, LLC will remain at 200 kw (at 70% load factor) of low cost power, and\nWHEREAS, the approval of the Virtual Polymer Compounds, LLC renewal application will not only maintain economic development in Niagara County, but also assist Virtual Polymer Compounds, LLC to remain competitive in the manufacturing of Primary Plastics, corrosion-free commercial fans, and InterActive Play divisions, which is the primary product lines at the Lockport plant, and\nWHEREAS, the Empower Niagara Board recommends the approval of the extension of low cost power allocation of200kw (at 70% load factor) for a period of three (3) years commencing February 1, 2025 to Virtual Polymer Compounds, LLC, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature hereby approves Virtual Polymer Compounds, LLC request for a three-year extension of low cost power benefits from Niagara County, pursuant to low cost power allocation received through the June 25, 2005 HCRSA and ASPNPPE, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the Empower User Agreement for Low Cost Power with Virtual Polymer Compounds, LLC for an extension of200 kw (at 70% load factor) oflow cost power for an additional three-year period.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","ED-004-25":"Resolution No. ED-004-25\nFrom: Economic Development Committee.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nAPPROVAL OF LOW COST POWER BENEFIT TO\nBIG DITCH BREWING COMPANY UNDER AGREEMENT FOR THE\nSALE AND PURCHASE OF NIAGARA PROJECT POWER & ENERGY (ASPNPPE)\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara entered into the Host Connnunity Relicensing and Settlement Agreement (\"HCRSA\") on June 25, 2005, and\nWHEREAS, the HCRSA entitles the County of Niagara to 9 megawatts of low cost power allocation (at 70% load) form the New York Power Authority, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara has made the use of this low cost power allocation for economic development purposes as the top priority for that allocation, and\nWHEREAS, there is presently an Agreement for the Sale and Purchase of Niagara Project Power and Energy (\"ASPNPPE\") that has been approved by the County of Niagara and NYPA and the Governor of the State of New York, and\nWHEREAS, the ASPNPPE specifically provides that the County of Niagara, could use its low cost power allocation for economic development purposes, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara has taken the necessary actions to fully implement the benefits contained in the HCRSA and the ASPNPPE, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara established the Empower Niagara Board by resolution ED-021-07 to review applications for the use of low cost power for economic development by reviewing applications and to make reconnnendation to Legislature of Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, the application of Big Ditch Brewing Company, a craft brewery and restaurant, located at the Historic Old Post Office in the City of Lockport, to open in February, 2025, has met and exceeded all the criteria qualifying its project for consideration and the Empower Niagara Board is reconnnending approval of this application, and\nWHEREAS, Big Ditch Brewing Company opened its headquarters in 2014 and a Tap Room in 2015 in the City of Buffalo and has grown to be Western New York's largest independently owned brewery and one of the 10 largest independently owned breweries in New York State, and\nWHEREAS, Big Ditch Brewing Company is investing $1.2 million to acquire and install machinery and equipment and to redesign the 15,000 square foot space at the Historic Old Post Office, serving as the company's \"Innovation Brewery,\" producing small batches of experimental products, which will later be scaled into bigger batches and larger volume products, and\nWHEREAS, the application was approved for 248 kw (at 70% load factor) of low cost power, which will allow Big Ditch Brewing Company to operate as craft brewery and restaurant at the Historic O Id Post Office in the City of Lockport, serving as the anchor tenant and employing 10 full-time employees, and\nWHEREAS, the approval of Big Ditch Brewing Company application for 248 kw (at 70% load factor) of low cost power will not only increase economic development in the City of Lockport and Niagara County, but will also assist Big Ditch Brewing Company to remain competitive in the craft brewery and restaurant business and will have a good economic effect on the connnunity in Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, the Empower Niagara Board will be reconnnending the approval of the application for low cost power allocation of248kw (at 70% load factor) for a period of three (3) years connnencing February 1, 2025,\nnow, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature hereby approves the application of Big Ditch Brewing Company for 248kw (at 70% load factor) low cost power benefits from Niagara County, pursuant to low cost power allocation received through the June 25, 2005 HCRSA and ASPNPPE, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute this Empower User Agreement for Low Cost Power with Big Ditch Brewing Company.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","ED-005-25":"Resolution No. ED-005-25\nFrom: Economic Development and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nNIAGARA COUNTY DEPARTMENT OF ECONOMIC DEVELOPMENT\nSOLE SOURCE STANDARDIZATION OF ON-AIR AND STREAMING RADIO MARKETING\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Department of Economic Development (NCDED) is tasked with helping to grow the county's tax base by assisting businesses located in Niagara County and attracting new companies to the region, and\nWHEREAS, during the last several years the department has utilized a variety of marketing resources to foster economic development throughout the county, and\nWHEREAS, NCDED has developed a working relationship with Audacy, Inc., and its local business and talk radio station WBEN-AM, and\nWHEREAS, Auducy, Inc. offers both on-air and streaming services that reach not only the Western New York area, but also into Southern Ontario, a key demographic for business attraction, and\nWHEREAS, through the development of ads and content, NCDED has been able to promote business opportunities to local companies and those looking to establish a location in Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, through marketing campaign analytics NCDED has tangible results that there is an increase in engagements with businesses of 15%+ during the times that on-air and streaming marketing was ongoing, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that for reasons of efficiency and economy, pursuant to General Municipal Law 103(5), there is a need for standardization for the Niagara County Department of Economic Development in the purchase of on-air and streaming radio campaigns, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that it is hereby determined that Auducy, Inc. is a sole source for on-air and streaming radio marketing, and that the Niagara County Department of Economic Development is hereby authorized to purchase on-air and streaming ad campaigns from Auducy, Inc. without the need to solicit for alternate proposals, such purchase to be made in consultation with Purchasing Department and in accordance with budgetary procedures.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-001-25":"Resolution No. IF-001-25\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nNIAGARA COUNTY SOLID WASTE - CLEAN ENERGY COMMUNITIES GRANT\nACCEPTANCE OF FUNDS\nWHEREAS, New York State Research and Development Authority (NYSERDA) has provided a grant to Niagara County for meeting certain criteria for the Clean Energy Communities Grant Program, and\nWHEREAS, the Phase II award is one hundred and twenty-five thousand dollars ($125,000), which was budgeted in the 2025 Budget process, and\nWHEREAS, the New York State Climate Act charged the Climate Justice Working with the development of criteria to identify disadvantaged communities to ensure that frontline and otherwise underserved communities benefit from the states transition to cleaner, greener sources of energy, reduced pollution and cleaner air, and economic opportunities, and\nWHEREAS, the New York State Research and Development Authority incentivizes communities to locate projects in a New York State Disadvantage Community by offering a 50% grant bonus, and\nWHEREAS, these funds will be used to finance electric vehicle charging infrastructure, electric landscaping equipment, and/or purchase two (2) electric vehicles for use in Disadvantaged Communities identified in Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, Niagara County may be eligible for an additional sixty-two thousand five hundred dollars $62,500, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE ANTICIPATED REVENUE:\nA.15.8160.802 43989.05  Other Home & Community Service Climate Conscious  $62,500\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.15.8160.802 72100.12  Machinery & Equipment Cars, Vans, Light Trucks  $62,500\nand it be further,\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the documents may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-002-25":"Resolution No. IF-002-25\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nNIAGARA COUNTY SOLID WASTE - CLEAN ENERGY COMMUNITIES GRANT\nWHEREAS, Resolution IF-183-24, dated November 12, 2024, accepted grant funds for meeting certain criteria for the Clean Energy Communities Grant Program, in the amount of $20,000, and\nWHEREAS, not all funds were expensed in 2024 and the 2025 Budget needs to be amended to make the grant funds available in the current year, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE ESTIMATED REVENUE:\nA.15.8160.802 43989.05  Other Home & Community Service  $3,133.41\nClimate Conscious\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.15.8160.802 74500.01  Contractual Expenses  $3,133.41\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-003-25":"Resolution No. IF-003-25\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nAGREEMENT BETWEEN THE COUNTY OF NIAGARA\nAND SCOUTING AMERICA TROOP 4\nWHEREAS, the Scouting America Troop 4 has requested that the County of Niagara grant them permission to use Burmaster Park on February 7th - 9th, 2025 for the purpose of holding a Winter Campout, and\nWHEREAS, this program benefits the residents of Niagara County as a whole, and\nWHEREAS, the event coordinator will provide all insurances required by the Risk Management Office, and agree to cover all extraordinary expenses associated with event, and\nWHEREAS, it is the desire of Niagara County to enter into a formal agreement with the Scouting America Troop 4,and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the agreement, the County Attorney will review the agreement for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the terms and conditions of the agreement between the County of Niagara and the Scouting America Troop 4 as appears on the proposed agreement, is hereby approved in all respects, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the agreement with Scouting America Troop 4's Winter Campout may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-004-25":"Resolution No. IF-004-25\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nNIAGARA COUNTY GOLF COURSE RA TES FOR 2025 SEASON\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Public Works Departments, is responsible for the daily operations of the Niagara County Golf Course on Davison Road, and\nWHEREAS, it is the goal of the department to keep the rates economical for users of the course, and\nWHEREAS, after a review of the current golf rates currently being charged at the Niagara County Golf Course, the department is recommending proposed increases for various fees, as per the rate chart, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Infrastructure and Facilities Committee has reviewed the proposed 2025 fee schedule and recommends the implementation of the 2025 Golf Course rates, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the proposed 2025 fee scheduled be implemented as of February 1, 2025.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-005-25":"Resolution No. IF-005-25\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nNIAGARA COUNTY PARK RATES FOR 2025 SEASON\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Public Works Departments, is responsible for the daily operations of the Niagara County Parks Department, and\nWHEREAS, it is the goal of the department to keep the shelter rates economical for users of the Parks, and\nWHEREAS, after a review of the current shelter rates and hall rental rates currently being charged for the various park facilities, the department is recommending proposed increases for various rental rates, as per the 2025 chart, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Infrastructure and Facilities Committee has reviewed the proposed 2025 shelter fee schedule and recommends the implementation of the 2025 Park Rental rates, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the proposed 2025 fee scheduled be implemented as of February 1, 2025.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-006-25":"Resolution No. IF-006-25\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nNIAGARA COUNTY PUBLIC WORKS\nSOLE SOURCE STANDARDIZATION NYSEG\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Public Works Department is starting a project to upgrade the electrical power to the Golf Course Facility, and\nWHEREAS, NYSEG is the only utility company which can provide electrical service in this area, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Public Works Department has received an estimate from NYSEG for the installation of poles at the Golf Course located at 314 Davison Road, Lockport, in an amount less than $11,000,\nnow, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that for reasons of efficiency and economy, pursuant to General Municipal Law 103(5), there is a need for sole source purchase and standardization for the Niagara County Public Works Department in the upgrade of electrical service to the Golf Course Facility on Davison Road, Lockport and future projects in which NYSEG is the power utility company for the area, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that it is hereby determined that NYSEG is the sole provider of electrical utility service for the Lockport area and other areas within Niagara County and that the Public Works Department is hereby authorized to purchase the electrical service work from NYSEG in all areas where NYSEG is the sole electrical utility company without the need to solicit for alternate proposals, and such purchase is to be made m consultation with the Purchasing Department and in accordance with budgetary procedures, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, all orders with NYSEG may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-007-25":"Resolution No. IF-007-25\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nTROTT ACCESS SIGNAGE CONTRACT\nCHANGE ORDER NO. 1\nWHEREAS, Niagara County Purchasing advertised for Bids for the Outdoor Electronic Message Sign for the Trott ACCESS Center, and\nWHEREAS, Cooper Neon Sign Co. Inc., 7350 Porter Road, Niagara Falls, NY 14304, was the sole bidder, for a cost of $60,228.60, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to modify the contract to allow for running and installing of conduit on the exterior building and drilling to accommodate the fiber optic cable to the third floor of Trott ACCESS Center, for a cost not to exceed $4,525.00, for a revised contract price of $64,753.60, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Change Order No. 1 to allow for the installation of conduit and drilling to accommodate the fiber optic cable to the third floor of Trott ACCESS Center be approved in the amount of $4,525.00 to Cooper Neon Sign Co. Inc., 7350 Porter Road, Niagara Falls, NY 14304, for a revised contract amount of $64,753.60, be approved, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Change Order may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-008-25":"Resolution No. IF-008-25\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nBRIDGE NY CULVERT LOCAL PROJECT AGREEMENT, PIN 5765.39\nROYALTON CENTER ROAD OVER MUD CREEK BRIDGE REPLACEMENT\nWHEREAS, the Royalton Center Road over Mud Creek Project, PIN 5765.39 (the \"Project\"), Town of Royalton, Niagara County, is eligible for funding under Title 23 U.S. Code, as amended, 23CFR as amended, and PUB.L.117-58, also known as the \"Bipartisan Infrastructure Law\" (BIL), and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara will design, let, and administer all phases of the Project, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara desires to advance the Project by making a commitment of 100% of the costs of the work for the Project or portions thereof, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Legislature of the County of Niagara hereby approves the above-subject project, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Legislature of the County of Niagara hereby authorizes the County of Niagara to pay 100% of the costs of the Engineering (Design I-VI) Phases of the Project or portions thereof, with the understanding that qualified costs may be eligible for federal aid, state aid, or reimbursement from Bridge NY funds, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to reflect the funding levels set forth by Schedule A in the attached agreement: INCREASE ESTIMATED REVENUE:\nH777.15.5197.000 44597.01  Fed Aid Cap Const Hwy Revenue  $313,500\nINCREASE ESTIMATED APPROPRIATIONS:\nH777.15.5197.000 72600.02  Infrastructure Bridges  $313,500\nand be it further\nRESOLVED, that the sum of $330,000 is hereby appropriated in account H777.15.5197.000 72600.02, Infrastructure Bridges, and is made available to cover the cost of participation in the above phases of the Project, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature hereby agrees that the County of Niagara shall be responsible for all costs of the Project, including costs which exceed the amount of reimbursement available from the Bridge NY funding awarded to the County of Niagara, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that in the event the Project costs exceed the amount appropriated above, the County of Niagara shall convene its Legislature as soon as possible to appropriate said excess amount immediately upon the notification by the New York State Department of Transportation, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the County of Niagara hereby agrees that construction of the Project shall begin no later than twenty-four (24) months after award and the construction phase of the Project shall be completed within thirty (30) months, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Chair of the Legislature of the County of Niagara be, and hereby is, authorized to execute all necessary Agreements, certifications, or reimbursement requests with NYSDOT for state aid and/or Bridge NY funding on behalf of the County of Niagara, in connection with the advancement or approval of the Project providing for the administration of the Project and the County of Niagara's funding of Project costs, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the County of Niagara will be responsible for all maintenance of the Project, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that a certified copy of this Resolution be filed with the New York State Commissioner of Transportation by attaching it to any necessary Agreement in connection with the Project, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the required documents, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that this Resolution shall take effect immediately.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-009-25":"Resolution No. IF-009-25\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nAW ARD CONSUL TANT CONTRACT - REHABILITATION OF FIVE BRIDGES\nWHEREAS, the Department of Public Works evaluated proposals from pre-qualified consulting engineering firms to assist the County with the Rehabilitation of Five Bridges Project, and\nWHEREAS, funds are available in account D.15.5120.000 74650.07, Professional Engineering Services, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the consultant services contract for the Rehabilitation of Five Bridges Project be awarded to Colliers Engineering and Design, 40 LaRiviere Drive, Suite 200, Waterfront Village Center, Buffalo, NY 14202, for a contract amount not to exceed $300,000, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Contract may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-010-25":"Resolution No. IF-010-25\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nA WARD CONSULTANT CONTRACT - PORTER CENTER ROAD FROM SWANN ROAD TO\nLAKE ROAD - ROAD AND DRAINAGE IMPROVEMENTS\nWHEREAS, the Department of Public Works evaluated proposals from pre-qualified consulting engineering firms to assist the County with the Porter Center Road from Swann Road to Lake Road - Road and Drainage Improvements Project, and\nWHEREAS, the East Canal Road Rehabilitation Project has been completed and there are remaining funds in this project, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modifications be effectuated to allow the project to proceed: DECREASE REVENUE:\nH731.15.5112.000 43501.00  Consol Hwy Aid Revenue  $100,000\nDECREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nH731.15.5112.000 72600.01  Roads  $100,000\nINCREASE REVENUE:\nH809.15.5112.000 43501.00  Consol Hwy Aid Revenue  $100,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nH809.15.5112.000 72600.01  Roads  $100,000\nand be it further\nRESOLVED, that the consultant services contract for survey work for the Porter Center Road from Swann Road to Lake Road - Road and Drainage Improvements Project be awarded to Frandina Engineering and Land Surveying, PC, 1701 Hertel Avenue, Buffalo, NY 14216, for a contract amount not to exceed $106,259.68, and\nbe it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Contract may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-011-25":"Resolution No. IF-011-25\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nJOHNSON CREEK ROAD BRIDGE OVER GOLDEN HILL CREEK BRIDGE REPLACEMENT\nCONSULTANT AMENDMENT NO. 3\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-102-23, dated June 20, 2023, awarded the contract for consultant services for the Johnson Creek Road Bridge over Golden Hill Creek Bridge Replacement Project to Mott MacDonald New York Inc., 438 Main Street, Suite 300, Buffalo, NY 14202, for a contract amount not to exceed $197,787, and\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-026-24, dated January 23, 2024, increased the contract in the amount of $3,280 for the preparation of a Bridge New York application for this bridge, for a revised contract amount of $201,067, and\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-097-24, dated May 14, 2024, extended the contract completion date to December 31, 2024, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to extend the contract completion date to December 31, 2026, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the contract to Mott MacDonald New York Inc., 438 Main Street, Suite 300, Buffalo, NY 14202, be extended to December 31, 2026, at no additional cost to the County, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Amendment may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-012-25":"Resolution No. IF-012-25\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nWILLOW ROAD BRIDGE OVER THE EAST BRANCH OF TWELVE MILE CREEK\nCONSULTANT AMENDMENT NO. 3\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-103-23, dated June 20, 2023, awarded the contract for consultant services for the Willow Road Bridge over the East Branch of Twelve Mile Creek Bridge Replacement Project to Mott MacDonald New York Inc., 438 Main Street, Suite 300, Buffalo, NY 14202, for a contract amount not to exceed $199,524, and\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-017-24, dated January 23, 2024, increased the contract in the amount of $3,280 for the preparation of a Bridge New York application for this bridge, for a revised contract amount of $202,804, and\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-098-24, dated May 14, 2024, extended the contract completion date to December 31, 2024, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to extend the contract completion date to December 31, 2026, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the contract to Mott MacDonald New York Inc., 438 Main Street, Suite 300, Buffalo, NY 14202, be extended to December 31, 2026, at no additional cost to the County, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Amendment may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-013-25":"Resolution No. IF-013-25\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nHARTLAND ROAD CULVERT NO.108-10\nCONSULTANT AMENDMENTNO.1\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-173-23, dated November 14, 2023, authorized the contract for consultant services for the Hartland Road Culvert No. 108-10 Project to Greenman-Pedersen, Inc., 4950 Genesee Street, Suite 100, Buffalo, NY 14225, for a contract amount not to exceed $60,000, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to increase the contract in the amount of $106,578 for construction inspection and construction administration, for a revised contract amount of $166,578, and\nWHEREAS, it is also necessary to extend the contract completion date to June 30, 2026, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to allow the project to proceed: INCREASE REVENUE:\nH798.15.5197.000 43501.00  Consol Hwy Aid Revenue  $75,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nH798.15.5197.000 72600.01  Infrastructure Roads  $75,000\nand be it further\nRESOLVED, that Amendment No. I to increase the contract by $106,578 for Greenman-Pedersen, Inc., 4950 Genesee Street, Suite 100, Buffalo, NY 14225, for the Hartland Road Culvert No. 108-10 Project, for a revised contract amount of $166,578, be approved, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the contract be extended to June 30, 2026, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Amendment may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-014-25":"Resolution No. IF-014-25\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nROAD EVALUATIONS AND PRIORITIZATION FOR TIP SUBMISSION\nAMENDMENT NO. 1 - FINAL\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-117-24, dated June 18, 2024, awarded the contract for the Road Evaluation and Prioritization for Tip Submission Project to Urban Engineers of NY, P.C., 403 Main Street, Suite 530, Buffalo, NY 14203 for a contract amount not to exceed $97,857, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to reduce the contract in the amount of $44,714.78, as the project is now complete, for a revised contract amount of$53,142.22, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Amendment. I-Final to decrease the contract by $44,714.78 for the Road Evaluation and Prioritization for Tip Submission Project, for a revised contract amount of $53,142.22, to Urban Engineers of NY, P.C., 403 Main Street, Suite 530, Buffalo, NY 14203, be approved, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that final payment be made to Urban Engineers of NY, P.C. as soon as practicable, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, Amendment No. I-Final may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-015-25":"Resolution No. IF-015-25\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nRECREATIONAL FACILITIES AT KRULL AND OPPENHEIM PARKS\nCONSULTANT AMENDMENT NO. 3\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-149-23, dated October 17, 2023, authorized the contract for consultant services for the Recreational Facilities at Krull and Oppenheim Parks Project to Wendel WD Architecture, Engineering, Surveying, and Landscape Architecture, PC, 237 Main Street, Suite 500, Buffalo, NY 14203, for a contract amount not to exceed $112,878, and\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-071-24 dated March 19, 2024 increased the contract in the amount of$9,790 for additional professional services, for a revised contract amount of$122,668, and\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-099-24 dated May 14, 2024 increased the contract in the amount of $3,000 for additional survey services to extend the survey area around the new pickle ball courts, to include the new parking area north of the courts, for a revised contract amount of$125,668, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to extend the contract completion date to December 31, 2025, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Amendment No. 3 to extend the contract completion date to December 31, 2025 for the Recreational Facilities at Krull and Oppenheim Parks Project, to Wendel WD Architecture, Engineering, Surveying, and Landscape Architecture, PC, be approved, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Amendment may be executed pursuant to tbe Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-016-25":"Resolution No. IF-016-25\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nEAST CANAL ROAD REHABILITATION AND DRAINAGE IMPROVEMENTS\nFROM ROBINSON ROAD TO FISK ROAD\nCHANGE ORDER NO. 1 - FINAL\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. CW-012-24, dated July 15, 2024, awarded the contract for the East Canal Road Rehabilitation and Drainage Improvements Project to Keeler Construction Co., 13519 West Lee Road, Albion, NY 14411, for a contract amount not to exceed $1,459,011.46, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to reduce the contract in the amount of$169,657.01, as the project is now complete, for a revised contract amount of$1,289,354.45, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Change Order No. 1-Final to decrease the contract by $169,657.01 for the East Canal Road Rehabilitation and Drainage Improvements Project, for a revised contract amount of $1,289,354.45, to Keeler Construction Co., 13519 West Lee Road, Albion, NY 14411, be approved, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that final payment be made to Keeler Construction Co. as soon as practicable, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, Change Order No. I-Final may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-017-25":"Resolution No. IF-017-25\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nEMERGENCY REPAIR WORK- BRALEY ROAD OVER THE EAST BRANCH OF\nTWELVE MILE CREEK BRIDGE RAIL PROJECT - CHANGE ORDER NO. 2\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-154-24, dated September 17, 2024, awarded the contract for emergency repair work at Braley Road over the East Branch of Twelve Mile Creek Bridge Rail Project to Concrete Applied Technologies Corp., d/b/a CATCO, 1266 Townline Road, Alden, NY 14004, for a contract amount not to exceed $200,000, and\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-217-24, dated December 3, 2024, extended the contract completion date to March !, 2025, at no additional cost to the County, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to extend the contract completion date to June 1, 2025, at no additional cost to the county, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Change Order No. 2 to extend the contract completion date to June 1, 2025 for the emergency repair work at Braley Road over the East Branch of Twelve Mile Creek Bridge Rail Project for Concrete Applied Technologies Corp., d/b/a CATCO, 1266 Townline Road, Alden, NY 14004, be approved, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Change Order may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-018-25":"Resolution No. IF-018-25\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nAUTHORIZATION TO APPLY FOR BUILD TRANSPORTATION DISCRETIONARY GRANT\nWHEREAS, Niagara County would like to take advantage of federal infrastructure funds targeted for rural projects to address multiple needs throughout Niagara County's rural road network, including bridges that are in disrepair from consistent farm-related activity and have not been upgraded or improved in decades, and\nWHEREAS, this initiative will package a number of bridge projects strategic to Niagara County's agribusiness network and are in the most need of improvements for consideration through the federal Better Utilizing Investments to Leverage Development (BUILD) Transportation Discretionary Grant Program, and\nWHEREAS, more than 40% of Niagara County's total acreage is dedicated to farms, representing more than $120 million in annual sales and employment for more than 1,000 people, and\nWHEREAS, per the FY 2025 Appropriations Act, BUILD Transportation Discretionary Grants may be used for funding of a project, and\nWHEREAS, Niagara County has needs for rural bridge improvements totaling $4 million, with a projected 10% match, or $400,000, to be funded with Consolidated Highway Improvement Program (CHIPS) Funds, and\nWHEREAS, applications for the FY 2025 Appropriations Act, BUILD Transportation Discretionary Grants are due by January 30, 2025, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the grant application, the County Attorney will review the agreement for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Garret Meal, Niagara County Commissioner of Public Works, is hereby authorized and directed to file an application for funds from the FY 2025 Appropriations Act, BUILD Discretionary Grant Program in the amount of $4 million for rural bridge improvements and is authorized to draw up a contract for this grant, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Contract may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-019-25":"Resolution No. IF-019-25\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nGENERAL CONSTRUCTION CONTRACT- NEW PUBLIC WORKS FACILITY\nA WARD TO NEXT LOWEST BIDDER\nWHEREAS, Resolution IF-222-24, dated December 3, 2024, awarded the General Construction Contract for the New Public Works Facility, to the apparent lowest, responsible bidder, Picone Construction Corp., 10995 Main Street, Clarence, NY 14031, in the amount of$9,220,000, and\nWHEREAS, the County has been working diligently to obtain the necessary signature on the contract documents, and\nWHEREAS, per attached letter the County could not obtain an executed contract with the low bidder, and\nWHEREAS, after discussion with the second lowest bidder, Walter S. Johnson Bldg. Co., PO Box 688, Niagara Falls, NY 14302, it has been confirmed that they will honor their original bid, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the General Construction Contract for the New Public Works Facility be awarded to Walter S. Johnson Bldg. Co., PO Box 688, Niagara Falls, NY 14302 in the amount of$9,760,000.00, and be it further\nMoved by Myers, second by Nemi to amend the resolution.\nSecond RESOLVED added: \" ... RESOLVED, that the execution of this contract by the Chairman of the Legislature is subject to the review of the County Attorney, County Manager and the County Treasurer .... \"\nMoved by Myers, second by Nemi to approve amendment.\nMoved by Myers, seconded by Nemi.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-020-25":"Resolution No. IF-020-25\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nDPW-HIGHWAY\nEXPENDITURE OF COUNTY ROAD FUND MONIES\nWHEREAS, the Commissioner of Public Works recommends the expenditure of County Road Fund monies for maintenance ofroads under Section 129 of the Highway Law in the sum of$9,723,863, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the necessary documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the recommendation of the Commissioner of Public Works be approved and the sum of $9,723,863 be appropriated from the County Road Fund for maintenance during the year 2025, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the required documents can be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-021-25":"Resolution No. IF-021-25\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nNIAGARA COUNTY HIGHWAY MAINTENANCE 2024 BUDGET MODIFICATION\nWHEREAS, the Highway Department allocates funds for Equipment Usage in the Highway Maintenance, Highway Bridge Maintenance, Highway Drainage, County Snow & Ice and State Snow & Ice activities, and\nWHEREAS, in 2024, the NYS Department of Transportation guided municipalities to begin using the FEMA equipment rate guide, and\nWHEREAS, in 2024, the Highway Department performed more maintenance activities than estimated during the 2024 budget process and additional funds are required in the equipment lease/rental budget line, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE APPROPRIATED FUND BALANCE:\nD.40599.00  Appropriated Fund Balance  $726,856\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nD.15.5110.000 74200.04  Equipment Lease/Rental  $726,856\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IL-001-25":"Resolution No. IL-001-25\nFrom: Legislators Richard E. Abbott, Anthony J. Nemi and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE LOCKPORT PUBLIC ARTS COUNCIL\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Lockport Public Arts Council is a 501 (c) (3) and their mission is to support numerous projects in the City of Lockport, and\nWHEREAS, one of the projects is \"The Secret Weapons of World War II\" to celebrate the 80th anniversary of the end of World War II, and\nWHEREAS, a 3-month exhibit will be displayed at the Kenan Center and Lockport Public Library, and\nWHEREAS, the exhibit will concentrate on the little-known role of woman, books and music during World War II, and\nWHEREAS, it will display authentic memorabilia highlighting Women of the Resistance, American Red Cross Club mobile girls, Armed Services books and rare recordings, and\nWHEREAS, this project will concentrate on bringing veterans and their families as well as local schools to this project, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the Lockport Public Arts Council by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership as follows:\nLockport Public Arts Council  $3,000.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IL-002-25":"Resolution No. IL-002-25\nFrom: Legislator Christopher Voccio and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF NIAGARA HERITAGE OF HOPE AND SERVICE (HOLY\nTRINITY) THROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Niagara Heritage of Hope and Service is a nonprofit organization that maintains the Holy Trinity historic complex on Falls Street in the City of Niagara Falls, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara Heritage of Hope and Service provides much needed services to the community in and beyond the Falls Street corridor, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara Heritage of Hope and Service has an urgent need of repair in its facilities, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the Niagara Heritage of Hope and Service by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows:\nNiagara Heritage of Hope and Service  $3,000.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IL-003-25":"Resolution No. IL-003-25\nFrom: Legislators Randy R. Bradt, Richard L. Andres and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE KNIGHTS OF COLUMBUS\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Knights of Columbus in North Tonawanda was established in 1924, and\nWHEREAS, the Knights of Columbus was founded on the principles of charity, unity and fraternity, and\nWHEREAS, the Knights of Columbus building is used for many functions such as banquets, weddings, bingo, etc. where these profits go to help those in need in the community, and\nWHEREAS, the Knights of Columbus are requesting funds to help with the cost of building upgrades, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the Knights of Columbus appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows:\nKnights of Columbus  $6,600.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IL-004-25":"Resolution No. IL-004-25\nFrom: Legislator Richard E. Abbott and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE BARGE CANAL OPTIMIST CLUB\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, Barge Canal Optimist Club was established in 1990 to promote good government and civic affairs through community activities and active community participation, and\nWHEREAS, Barge Canal Optimist is hosting their 26th Annual Tee Off Against Breast Cancer \"For the Love of Clara\" on June 2nd at Willowbrook Golf Course in Lockport NY, and\nWHEREAS, Barge Canal Optimist Club is raising funds for the Breast Cancer Network of WNY and BCOC Youth Fund via the golf tournament and other fundraising activities, and\nWHEREAS, Barge Canal Optimist Club will provide volunteers to operate the tournament, and\nWHEREAS, Barge Canal Optimist Club will require promotional aids and publicity to promote this endeavor,\nnow, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports Barge Canal Optimist Club appropriating momes from said Community Partnership Program Fund as follows:\nBarge Canal Optimist Club  $1,000.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IL-005-25":"Resolution No. IL-005-25\nFrom: Legislator Richard E. Abbott and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF LOCKPORT IN BLOOM\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, Lockport in Bloom is a not-for-profit organization, chartered in 2005, that promotes the beautification of Lockport by encouraging residents to share with others their beautiful and creative gardens, and\nWHEREAS, by encouraging neighborhood beautification, Lockport in Bloom helps promote community pride by bringing people to the area to view the many beautiful gardens, and\nWHEREAS, through their annual Garden Festival, Lockport in Bloom brings thousands of visitors to the City of Lockport to view the 40 plus gardens that are put on display every summer, and\nWHEREAS, local businesses and restaurants benefit greatly from the increase in foot traffic that Lockport in Bloom generates throughout the City of Lockport, and\nWHEREAS, Niagara County is interested in supporting efforts that generate increased revenue for businesses and create a sense of pride throughout the community, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports Lockport in Bloom by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Program Fund as follows:\nLockport in Bloom  $1,500.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IL-006-25":"Resolution No. IL-006-25\nFrom:  Legislators Richard E. Abbott, Anthony J. Nemi and Carla L. Speranza and Economic Development\nCommittee.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF LOCKPORT LOCKS HERITAGE DISTRICT CORPORATION\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, 2025 marks the 200th Anniversary of the Erie Canal and Flight of Five Locks, and\nWHEREAS, the Lockport Locks Heritage District Corporation is sponsoring the production of the documentary America's Stairway, and\nWHEREAS, the documentary will be highlighted during the World Canals Conference in September 2025, and\nWHEREAS, the Lockport Locks Heritage District Corporation is requesting Community Partnership Funds to enable the completion and distribution costs for the documentary, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports Lockport Locks Heritage District appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Program Fund as follows:\nLockport Locks Heritage District Corporation  $10,000.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IL-007-25":"Resolution No. IL-007-25\nFrom: Legislators Richard E. Abbott and Anthony J. Nemi and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: January 21, 2025\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE ARTS AT LOCKPORT SCHOOL DISTRICT THROUGH THE\nUSE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Arts program in the Lockport School District promotes musical plays through participation by students and teachers, and\nWHEREAS, the Arts has scheduled a musical production of Mary Poppins, and\nWHEREAS, the Arts is seeking monies to offset the cost of costumes for the Mary Poppins musical, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the Arts at Lockport School district by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Program Fund as follows:\nThe Arts LSHS Musical Lockport School District  $1,000.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent."};
